#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415441 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f77ceb7-6be8-5d0b-93e7-60790b6d14db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415407} {:answer/id #uuid "488a4e09-5667-54f6-ad88-5c61e9a4ef4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415411} {:answer/id #uuid "65db0f0e-cfd6-52b8-9117-b967fb489769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415415} {:answer/id #uuid "8b52cac3-7234-5848-87cf-921935519eb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415421} {:answer/id #uuid "9b98bb58-d459-5b8d-8729-a3f1ab440861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415423}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415407} :answer-field/id #uuid "79e92368-7b49-5e53-95cb-a87e9c528421" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "36f8b820-02e2-5d5e-8155-bd2e7c9da1db" :question/math-academy-id "q-124660" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to the point $B(1,1),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y\\,dx-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=0\\,i+0\\,j$ and $b=i+j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =0\\,i+0\\,j+t(i+j-(0\\,i+0\\,j)) \\\\  & =t\\,i+t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=t$ and $y(t)=t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y\\,dx-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\cdot 1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\cdot 1\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t}{\\pi }-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t}{\\pi }]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\pi }[-1+0-1-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415442 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "225888f9-79d1-51ec-b69e-df715e48a48c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415403} {:answer/id #uuid "3341cdec-88e2-5336-a6c4-1be4742e22df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{22}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415408} {:answer/id #uuid "5b61e1f7-a8be-5897-ab62-f5700cfc5da5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415414} {:answer/id #uuid "7872d0b7-7e4c-5866-a2c5-fa62a0852ac3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415418} {:answer/id #uuid "ff788f7f-efa0-5800-af8c-7f4c47429255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415432}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415432} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac11d5c1-82f1-564e-be01-8e20a8e6d4bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4ee6b1fc-a5dc-5685-b3c9-ad25e0e1efc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-124666" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y={x}^{2}-1$ traversed from the point $P(1,0)$ to point $Q(2,3),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+x\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y={x}^{2}-1.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+({t}^{2}-1)\\,j,\\,t\\in [1,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}({y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{({t}^{2}-1)}^{2}\\cdot 1+t\\cdot 2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{4}-2{t}^{2}+1+2{t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{4}+1\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}+t]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{2}^{5}}{5}+2]-[\\frac{{1}^{5}}{5}+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{5}+2-\\frac{1}{5}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415443 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29488f70-3dff-5945-99fa-114e41ce2085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415405} {:answer/id #uuid "38c233c1-8522-530d-9bbc-48e152799452" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415409} {:answer/id #uuid "794f752f-1659-5318-a69d-58c5a353960e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415419} {:answer/id #uuid "d6e2e962-8b22-57c7-9fde-b02c03d0533e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415425} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ac4b86-8657-5c33-ab2c-ac749e9ddbc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415428}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415409} :answer-field/id #uuid "b523b71f-1a4d-563c-8ba4-de711d207cf0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415437}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b73ad7ec-dc24-5c99-a917-9ce5919adbcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-90714" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{FG}$ traversed from the point $F(1,0)$ to the point $G(2,3),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}x\\,dx-xy\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i$ and $b=2\\,i+3\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =i+t(2\\,i+3\\,j-i) \\\\  & =i+t(i+3\\,j) \\\\  & =(1+t)\\,i+3t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=1+t$ and $y(t)=3t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+3\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x\\,dx-xy\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+t)\\cdot 1-(1+t)\\cdot 3t\\cdot 3\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}1-8t-9{t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[t-4{t}^{2}-3{t}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[1-4(1)^{2}-3(1)^{3}]-[0] \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415770 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "126d13a7-71fa-5051-a4a2-28f29e56cf03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415734} {:answer/id #uuid "2216ca5d-6c6a-5107-a068-323114e3fc15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415735} {:answer/id #uuid "7aae6b46-36e9-538a-8e22-b5381b749d2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415746} {:answer/id #uuid "7e44c518-6f87-55b7-bbc1-4434ed19cb47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415747} {:answer/id #uuid "8ea13c33-b0aa-51a8-a9cd-a56c81fb144a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415749}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415747} :answer-field/id #uuid "7adcd9af-5265-5496-ada0-3911ecad9cdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3412530f-81b3-51bb-95a9-4599a55eb207" :question/math-academy-id "q-77250" :question/problem "![](images/87/87182d625f4e669a6b2791a17f3e12c34922043b8f3deefd6b2473e8fb529886.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(2,0),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a section of the parabola $y=4-{x}^{2}$ from the point $A(2,0)$ to the point $B(0,4).$\n\nGiven that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2,\n$$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nLet ${C}_{2}$ be the section of parabola $y=4-{x}^{2}$ from $A$ to $B.$ Then,\n $-{C}_{2}$ will be the same path but travelled from $B$ to $A.$ The path $-{C}_{2}$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}(t)=t\\,i+(4-{t}^{2})\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<2.$ Along this curve, we have $x=t$ and $y=4-{t}^{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t)) & =x\\,i=t\\,i.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4-{t}^{2})j \\\\  & =i-2t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =(t\\,i)\\cdot (i-2t\\,j) \\\\  & =t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =2-{\\int }_{0}^{2}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2-{\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2-{[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2-[\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}] \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415771 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57f5be3d-e9a5-5b71-9237-641c57905868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415742} {:answer/id #uuid "6f4582a3-9b84-5136-bbc8-4a5b81e6b276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415744} {:answer/id #uuid "8d876fc2-4110-59e7-a123-efe4ad754f58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415748} {:answer/id #uuid "9fd91543-0a88-52f5-89ca-61af0000467f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415751} {:answer/id #uuid "ba144e5b-e62a-51ae-8288-ad68474c5bd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415753}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415751} :answer-field/id #uuid "16807e0b-25b3-557d-839e-c6e7045de982" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4ab286f6-317f-53cd-ac02-bf3eb20fe604" :question/math-academy-id "q-77148" :question/problem "![](images/0c/0c3b1312e7368d8fc760a274a49280b10d4571a06ee6237b28fc8a8b1b7316d3.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $DEFD,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the **clockwise** direction?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed in clockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}\\cup {-{C}_{3}},\n$$\n\nwhere $-{C}_{3}$ is the same path as ${C}_{3}$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415772 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "248f19de-9914-5bab-a8d3-9da16c44f98d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415736} {:answer/id #uuid "46aa7018-0782-59bf-8f3c-c481140a5532" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415739} {:answer/id #uuid "d3414a07-8bc9-5a0b-8216-6eb6ba5bc40b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415754} {:answer/id #uuid "e26dd6a1-e2c4-5310-904f-4261a100d96e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415757} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ecc9b3-f68e-5629-8e61-d1c693d7d7ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415761}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415739} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c4c4c60-58a2-5309-91fd-f009939a8cdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6da1b103-36df-5031-b2ef-46cb571416a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-77134" :question/problem "![](images/74/746a05b2345e50173ed898b741944ba435d17a44dfc075848be9d8fcd2f2ad90.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $PQRP,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the counterclockwise direction?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed in counterclockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC=\\{-{C}_{1}\\}\\cup {C}_{2}\\cup {C}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $-{C}_{1}$ is the same path as ${C}_{1}$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415773 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44901697-c22c-53ba-bb65-14f1081533bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12z\\,j-4\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415738} {:answer/id #uuid "49a222b7-ced0-5334-a476-927827cacddd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(y-z)x\\,i+2{z}^{3}\\,j-2{y}^{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415740} {:answer/id #uuid "6829492d-3f92-5206-8c0f-7015db0fd5bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(y-z)\\,i+6{z}^{2}\\,j-4y\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415743} {:answer/id #uuid "d7e04aa2-786d-5004-9af7-6898c9098f86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}∇(6{z}^{2}-z-2y)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415755} {:answer/id #uuid "ecda143d-5ef3-59db-8104-b35b72d657b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∇(6{z}^{2}-z-2y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415760}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415743} :answer-field/id #uuid "f968e9bb-1698-556c-94c3-47e9ed5d0dfa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6fc12b03-7934-57bc-b6a6-b1ef9ff5ade7" :question/math-academy-id "q-125425" :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(y-z)\\,\\text{d}x+6{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-4y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$ is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ for some piecewise smooth curve $C,$ then $F(x,y,z)=$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)\\,i+Q(x,y,z)\\,j+R(x,y,z)\\,k\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y+R\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nIn our case, $P=y-z,Q=6{z}^{2},R=-4y.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}((y-z)\\,i+6{z}^{2}\\,j-4y\\,k)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}(y-z)\\,\\text{d}x+6{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-4y\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $F=(y-z)\\,i+6{z}^{2}\\,j-4y\\,k.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415774 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55fc80b9-6193-508c-b227-e04b2840c9d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{51}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415741} {:answer/id #uuid "7527daea-f713-54e2-9b27-ffd3fc3bb110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{112}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415745} {:answer/id #uuid "e0ded463-bc16-568f-8c0b-62a6554bc47d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415756} {:answer/id #uuid "e3cf1e9e-5de9-575b-be80-c45bb969b3f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{23}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415758} {:answer/id #uuid "ff980988-6413-52f6-84a6-6d1daa6d7016" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415763}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415745} :answer-field/id #uuid "b523b1e1-5cbd-5efb-b1af-a137b9b5cd25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8399ab15-70cf-5db8-b1d1-61088de736be" :question/math-academy-id "q-77244" :question/problem "![](images/b4/b4ce481ac8babb7a3e5707f1ef44417b12e73abe4b4c2143c19b45332642c6d2.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment  from the point $B(0,8)$ to the point $A(-2,8),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a section of the curve $y=-{x}^{3}$ from the point $A(-2,8)$ to the point $O(0,0).$\n\nGiven that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=16,\n$$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=xy\\,i+y\\,j$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the section of the curve $y=-{x}^{3}$ from $A$ to $O.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}(t)=t\\,i-{t}^{3}\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $-2\\le t\\le 0.$ Along this curve, we have $x=t$ and $y=-{t}^{3}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t)) & =xy\\,i+y\\,j \\\\  & =-{t}^{4}\\,i-{t}^{3}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{t}^{3})j \\\\  & =i-3{t}^{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =(-{t}^{4}\\,i-{t}^{3}\\,j)\\cdot (i-3{t}^{2}\\,j) \\\\  & =-{t}^{4}+3{t}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =16+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(-{t}^{4}+3{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16+{[-\\frac{1}{5}{t}^{5}+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{6}]}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =16+[0-(-\\frac{(-{2}^{5})}{5}+\\frac{(-2)^{6}}{2})] \\\\  & =16-\\frac{192}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{112}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415775 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3124c0f4-4c6e-55ec-9349-8032b33b382f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{y-1}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415737} {:answer/id #uuid "990826b9-b6bc-588a-80e6-36af02d8f2ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415750} {:answer/id #uuid "b1e72ea2-4855-5111-a3dd-cd04fb8b2403" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415752} {:answer/id #uuid "e80ae482-54b0-51f8-b405-74f535b48c44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{e}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{x-1}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415759} {:answer/id #uuid "f546e017-61e1-549c-83d5-ab9a05185633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415762}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415762} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a4c61a4-7435-599c-afcf-0efe6b1cbdb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a47f5f1d-1328-52e8-8c90-bfca32946e28" :question/math-academy-id "q-125419" :question/problem "Let $F(x,y)={e}^{y}\\,i+{e}^{x}\\,j$ and let $C$ be the path defined by $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,t-1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1].$ Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{C}P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417343 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49e846d3-cfa4-53b9-966f-e4adab0cb729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/1d/1d502f64bd89571fcd7ccb43e339b0fb8554ebf2072d48eaf10829f96f9c170e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417331} {:answer/id #uuid "acf72fee-ab09-5c2f-a567-2d99703c6cb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ea/eac1fe8c8dee7394a49603187829adcc0fa81665e0eafb2674f43ebdf4493f10.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417334} {:answer/id #uuid "b10e879c-3c4a-5dd0-9361-bbc17fe37c13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/5d/5d27f4544c601da995ad2c1402547a477a244a6b79bff4e8b79f945299b73b87.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417335} {:answer/id #uuid "cf5ff7a9-5c75-54e7-82a4-5ae809e4f3a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e4/e40599ebb674fe5fe05c60be75f148f41458469ae6926c3388675ecdb24f66cf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417337} {:answer/id #uuid "d640a56e-f8d2-517d-a8ab-e60c9f56c036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/59/59bf689a32a8a0c5d347f29065acea60194b9f4b0fad084108ecb657257cd939.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417338}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417337} :answer-field/id #uuid "b10e9eb4-ee70-5020-8593-2fc167326803" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d04f0b91-140d-549f-8f9d-31d1d9b6c7bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-122758" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=-x\\,i-y\\,j$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(1,4),(2,-2),$ and $(4,-2).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=-x\\,i-y\\,j\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1,4) & =-(1)\\,i-4\\,j=-i-4\\,j \\\\ F(2,-2) & =-(2)\\,i-(-2)\\,j=-2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ F(4,-2) & =-(4)\\,i-(-2)\\,j=-4\\,i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/e4/e40599ebb674fe5fe05c60be75f148f41458469ae6926c3388675ecdb24f66cf.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417356 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e0d4b0b-8093-5290-b6b4-484e2a3c019b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417350} {:answer/id #uuid "3f52c558-a8f4-5f12-84e4-136a7b9663ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417351} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3faf64-35fa-5814-9c5b-2c546aef7f2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417352} {:answer/id #uuid "c802b759-c3a3-5234-a525-52e3dcf0d733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417353} {:answer/id #uuid "df929076-a9d7-5c37-b534-2a48f5c0f2ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417354}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417353} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6520f4c-cce8-5e56-adce-0289e4c3517a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2756cd36-f06f-563c-8e51-e0b858d03f97" :question/math-academy-id "q-160387" :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3{u}^{2}+2v \\\\ u+2{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=3{u}^{2}+2v$ and $y(u,v)=u+2{v}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =6u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =4v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}6u & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6u\\cdot 4v-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =24uv-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(1,1)}=24(1)(1)-2=22.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418478 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10989bff-b98e-51af-bb4a-b28350a226e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418467} {:answer/id #uuid "1687e4a9-1caf-5ff9-a03a-04a61daf8637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418468} {:answer/id #uuid "3d279b80-6077-585e-b090-d7cf3aa7efe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418469} {:answer/id #uuid "60f276b3-36f3-5fbe-9d10-b5fec8b429a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418470} {:answer/id #uuid "b1865271-a8a9-5f44-b938-a958ba7e5f06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418472}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418469} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ec0f1fd-a976-5791-b23e-66968bb1089c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d41258e7-4727-5661-9adb-26ceb25d76d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-161185" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}(xy+xz)\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,2v,\\,6v⟩\n$$\n\n \nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=2\\sqrt{10}.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=2\\sqrt{10}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(xy+xz)\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y=2v\\,z=6v} \\\\  & =(u)(2v)+(u)(6v) \\\\  & =8uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,8uv\\cdot 2\\sqrt{10}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =16\\sqrt{10}{∬}_{D}\\,uv\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =16\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}uv\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =16\\sqrt{10}({\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\,\\text{d}u)({\\int }_{0}^{2}v\\,\\text{d}v) \\\\  & =16\\sqrt{10}\\cdot {[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =16\\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =16\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "059c12fe-eab5-50d3-812e-daede28e5fce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-uv-v)\\sqrt{1+4{v}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418466} {:answer/id #uuid "a69adeb6-2c36-5a75-b29d-f701876ba59a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-u{v}^{2}+v)\\sqrt{1+4{v}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418471} {:answer/id #uuid "cbbd9a36-5d62-5a45-a0b6-211a1e349cc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(u{v}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{v}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418473} {:answer/id #uuid "ded9ba33-54e3-5794-92a9-b6db153210e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-u{v}^{2}-v)\\sqrt{1+4{v}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418474} {:answer/id #uuid "e264331f-dc93-54c6-bb93-c8cd29731c14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-u{v}^{2}+{v}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{v}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418475}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418474} :answer-field/id #uuid "de80caed-f292-5fbc-ae6d-5bf6abf46eda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418477}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e6c3f387-d028-56cc-8550-337dfde31023" :question/math-academy-id "q-161135" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=xz+y$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,-v,\\,-{v}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{1+4{v}^{2}}.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{1+4{v}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(xz+y)\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y=-v\\,z={v}^{2}} \\\\  & =u\\cdot (-{v}^{2})+(-v) \\\\  & =-u{v}^{2}-v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(-u{v}^{2}-v)\\sqrt{1+4{v}^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419401 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1796399c-8c6d-5f22-bede-36efd1a03f5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "uv\\sqrt{{u}^{2}{v}^{2}+4{v}^{4}+4{u}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419395} {:answer/id #uuid "8e82d031-e34f-52ca-aadf-8e8d59c47bb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(uv+{v}^{2})\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}{v}^{2}+4{v}^{4}+4{u}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419396} {:answer/id #uuid "bdf13d7f-d54d-59f5-9209-239de83443e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(v+{v}^{2})\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}{v}^{2}+4{v}^{2}+4{u}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419397} {:answer/id #uuid "bf1e4b2c-7a9b-5b87-a6e2-85dd90a414fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(uv+{u}^{2})\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}{v}^{2}+4{v}^{2}+4{u}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419398} {:answer/id #uuid "d04576fc-a1ab-5d09-bca6-54a80b70447c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{v}^{2}\\sqrt{{u}^{2}{v}^{2}+4{v}^{4}+4{u}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419399}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419396} :answer-field/id #uuid "39e51a49-d535-578a-8167-c77ca61f8305" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4970a05a-3173-5c58-8ea3-194b8fc59474" :question/math-academy-id "q-161133" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=x+z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨uv,\\,{u}^{2},\\,{v}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}{v}^{2}+4{v}^{4}+4{u}^{4}}.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}{v}^{2}+4{v}^{4}+4{u}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(x+z)\\,{|}_{x=uv\\,y={u}^{2}\\,z={v}^{2}} \\\\  & =uv+{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(uv+{v}^{2})\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}{v}^{2}+4{v}^{4}+4{u}^{4}}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420496 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aa1db5b-3f41-577b-8ee0-5f7105de71c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420472} {:answer/id #uuid "0c5de29c-bfc1-53ce-83a7-41993dcf260c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420473} {:answer/id #uuid "615383ae-5073-5a27-a6bc-b222285b07e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420480} {:answer/id #uuid "7860d733-39e7-5d34-930a-375944911a4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420483} {:answer/id #uuid "a9e0b03b-9a26-5f35-8452-4cc76772c98a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420486}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420483} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc977685-7a6f-5892-9d86-65a9ccbd87da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6071adb1-29d3-5cda-a599-699b41d64b95" :question/math-academy-id "q-226151" :question/problem "![](images/de/de3e018fa1382ee2250d1b2e256e3b47cafef346852007f0e6afb84342d7cb04.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=3x\\,i-2y\\,j$ and a rectangle $C$ with base $4$ and height $6$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/09/0950c559d5d6df93cf40afd8fdbd07f090f864fa462083bfd496246e7f2bded9.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(3x\\,i-2y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=2i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =3{\\int }_{-3}^{3}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=36.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(3x\\,i-2y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+3j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =-2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}3\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=-24.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 36+2\\cdot (-24)=24.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420909 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64bb62e3-bd57-57a6-9c6c-17776849a043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-x{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420872} {:answer/id #uuid "6e5de1a3-32b4-5e14-81b4-8c30c59f2613" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{5}{y}^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420873} {:answer/id #uuid "794f9e41-d152-5887-a25c-abf259a83c57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420877} {:answer/id #uuid "cf225ba4-1606-5bb5-954a-0c37ef35f638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420890} {:answer/id #uuid "cf5e5cf8-0499-54ef-8365-3f3efa38105a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420891}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420873} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4055bff-eb04-5d8c-8a7f-fd7faf3e0c1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "07e888b6-1046-5ef1-87ef-0610d3939941" :question/math-academy-id "q-127058" :question/problem "![](images/65/653699a9c78577272425de7f8e6d015743e2c2e8c6e9a21bef189dc06fd80684.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{5}{y}^{7}\\,i+x{y}^{3}\\,j$ and the rectangle $C$ shown above. If the flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{1}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $x$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $n=i.$\n\n![](images/35/35e33731fbe85305a4308ef03c9f793b77558d66350d9345c30e643df1c40ba1.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}({x}^{5}{y}^{7}\\,i+x{y}^{3}\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}{x}^{5}{y}^{7}\\,\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{5}{y}^{7}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420910 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1e6a479-52d1-5ae4-939a-e3b5d03fd778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474284} {:answer/id #uuid "4890dcc8-ff49-5248-a3fb-0fe1da9aeb4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474285}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474284} :answer-field/id #uuid "f527906f-2d30-55d6-998b-e4e8bf90fc09" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $C$ is negative" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475068} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb84b4f7-0bc5-5c5a-9056-122bbc70bc57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474286} {:answer/id #uuid "cc9a6aa9-acc4-5992-a14e-804d5a9c01a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474287}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474286} :answer-field/id #uuid "65cb578f-78b5-5225-966a-db5a469b668e" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475069} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fac217d6-6ce8-5003-9d34-2c9ea1c6c89d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474288} {:answer/id #uuid "bcc8acfa-931e-5f66-bebf-19feca6c0ef3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474289}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474289} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d35692f-6bb0-56b8-9a68-82c51fb3c32c" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0ae03bac-e2a8-5c3b-a5da-8c8f23590a03" :question/math-academy-id "q-127026" :question/problem "![](images/28/28a7eb32cfcd800c907e9f4af927eca7bd8616d36162682413db62a9feabefbb.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the clockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is negative” and “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero” are false. From the diagram below it's clear that there is a net flow *out* through $C.$ \n\n![](images/fe/fecf59674259d41222ffd981b1ab6d90c474fe2b0e37a6d5f7626f34536a6896.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive” is true. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/56/56a7fc09ed2a2a15b0949fa6041dc9c0ee6beb69f30b1a5c048afd8c57887d52.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420911 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2126782-b8eb-539b-8a44-4b656f8cdc89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474278} {:answer/id #uuid "bc560dd6-7ac6-5a76-9df4-70c53f25e56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474279}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474278} :answer-field/id #uuid "94621230-a9f0-50e7-b575-e0567a8c5e06" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475065} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43e5a016-6e60-5487-bdda-a02e34603edd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474280} {:answer/id #uuid "52b18bb0-cfb3-5c9f-948f-ea3749f36b0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474281}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474281} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5d370b0-1e92-5c62-966f-578bba64458f" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $C$ is negative" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475066} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "137d1b3d-a1ad-5c7d-a87f-d48c60a7cbf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474282} {:answer/id #uuid "6c50f32b-3afd-545c-a6cd-342f755fc462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474283}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474282} :answer-field/id #uuid "3241e849-1133-58ff-a9e9-26f068ba66c0" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1cc7cc51-6281-53f8-b74f-55c2a0b2fa6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-127024" :question/problem "![](images/01/01d8d85064253b9241b1fd93c22381897f23556d6e02c5c2c0161c94cee3bbdf.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero” is false, while statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is negative” is true. From the diagram below it's clear that there is a net flow *in* through $C.$ \n\n![](images/bb/bb4fd4068760bb9132052b26508f0e1e0c02c45d425770de804b17ce7c512919.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux is negative, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive” is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/46/46afd4cde01c95388c9daefbef0668ef696d5817ab9ea3614982edf58a9bdf55.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is negative” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420912 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86148950-3fc2-5f38-81ce-53ec93e76166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474290} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c0a1d4-d321-51db-9f9a-4313b742971e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474291}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474290} :answer-field/id #uuid "eff65640-e27c-5010-a702-518eac2480f9" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475071} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa09f0c0-3016-5c6e-b7c4-55a245d9d0ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474292} {:answer/id #uuid "d0c0e836-a991-5f1a-91a3-7effd835eb73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474293}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474293} :answer-field/id #uuid "176d727d-d760-55bb-bfd3-626eda4dc691" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475072} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7817b13e-8e9a-530f-860c-d01aa3b29a65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474294} {:answer/id #uuid "419f49db-2832-5c8c-8fb3-49f7638b7dd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474295}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474295} :answer-field/id #uuid "912976bb-a0fa-5d56-b533-8354e5ec824a" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1e18756f-1e50-5f09-be71-26f001c4f68e" :question/math-academy-id "q-127027" :question/problem "![](images/af/afaec113101f1a63134d2b1cb5c9744b93f9b84dabafaa205780d2dc5b49a7bc.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the clockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero” is false, while statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive” is true. From the diagram below it's clear that there is a net flow *out* through $C.$ \n\n![](images/6b/6b24ece279cc480f20d7a4dbc65f7a5257af71f22688b1416c73e3b40673a321.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive” is true. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/9e/9e8527d26f0f2bd5372d8643f8f9321ec022bb0079e1fc5b23be4978f436613c.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive”; “The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02b163d2-8d88-51ac-abaa-42c491e74881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420855} {:answer/id #uuid "0caccbf9-4642-59fa-8f70-8c1bac70ef51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420858} {:answer/id #uuid "1148ef69-d315-5d49-9d48-0b6ccac5465c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-y)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420860} {:answer/id #uuid "d961af8e-4079-56ce-98d9-0bbc2b4be0c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420894} {:answer/id #uuid "fb0b8003-7828-51fb-91cc-f49acd49d338" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-(x-y)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420899}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420860} :answer-field/id #uuid "544df328-c7fc-55b8-b971-da80f2afa474" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "71fbebcb-93ff-5be9-84c7-d1676b609367" :question/math-academy-id "q-127059" :question/problem "![](images/1b/1b08e4cc9dd6ebde7f4bf544d7a76c4706dbd69a2c31d4c0fda8f816d4f871b5.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)=(x+y)^{2}\\,i+(x-y)^{2}\\,j$ and the rectangle $C$  shown above. If the flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{2}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $y$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $n=j.$ \n\n![](images/5d/5d0e9699f75e1f3f727c5c771fdce1a66311496cb55d31c0aac789901feed0cb.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}((x+y)^{2}\\,i+(x-y)^{2}\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(x-y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $(x-y)^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420914 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fc352a7-def2-5836-9338-8008f169268f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474296} {:answer/id #uuid "3d7131d4-fc3c-5f44-9a24-a7c8c38852ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474297}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474297} :answer-field/id #uuid "72b3e34c-4c68-5a6b-8479-025af4a4eb97" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475074} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "367f3fa0-3258-53ba-b118-21195c91f07c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474298} {:answer/id #uuid "8135c933-85bc-5657-aea9-2ece1f6a47c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474299}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474298} :answer-field/id #uuid "91409016-0963-5d73-b7be-10a1f16408c7" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475075} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd98a563-23aa-5acb-89d3-93294ee5cc96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474300} {:answer/id #uuid "60ef0350-a526-513b-87b0-99da9194b0ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474301}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474300} :answer-field/id #uuid "17b07bc2-ae25-563c-8ed2-caa1721146f6" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "765f030b-c4cb-527c-8c26-c947b0ace6a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-127028" :question/problem "![](images/0f/0f1d25942ae680c068b587f38bfbc968c8850b8fa2db2003e994e1ec32dad892.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the clockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero” is true, while statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive” is false. Note that the flux $F$ that flows in through $C$ is the same that flows out through $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $F$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/b7/b7acc3028de66ae71ae4e425da639e51a357a77f2b0a345290592cac91ba17d3.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative” is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/04/04639a0074aa4607fa57bfe589aad1780f8da6f573d8589a68c2fdb569b5a129.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420915 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47a0ff6d-e708-565d-a280-3cadd01ffb31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "256" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420867} {:answer/id #uuid "7d25776a-11ff-529c-8f88-125b184a30fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420878} {:answer/id #uuid "d86896fc-5499-5228-a1a9-ce525916fc2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420893} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc47f6e-dbd1-5cc9-912f-6e7a2036089f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420895} {:answer/id #uuid "f9651ba3-7ceb-59ea-8fd1-b5bacff9edfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420898}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420893} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd37f018-666a-5dbb-8a05-7813cb84c875" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420908}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7f447fce-ea84-5549-9b80-d5d43d163662" :question/math-academy-id "q-226132" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f413fd04759cdb02118b320fea2f678712681a57f5b08e1bcb57a6843c0c80f0.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=4x\\,i+4y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $6$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/87/878a9992405e5968a03a04d12f42aeb85bca52c07dc7081244b04011643a74bf.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(4x\\,i+4y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =4{\\int }_{-3}^{3}3\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12(3-(-3)) \\\\  & =12\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =72.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=4\\cdot 72=288.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420916 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10472c8b-0f2c-541f-a4f7-87022439fc30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420859} {:answer/id #uuid "57aeb9a4-34ac-5fdf-99f7-f349f66e9ca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420869} {:answer/id #uuid "721de4ee-1215-587c-a230-6395ae1c468d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420874} {:answer/id #uuid "83ba3fd3-59b5-593e-9d3a-53cc62cc9e50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420879} {:answer/id #uuid "8fa05c49-f0b6-5d89-a641-050254ac8771" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420881}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420869} :answer-field/id #uuid "a01086e0-9b32-569e-ab08-30e8248e546d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ab737fa4-bfd8-53e2-9695-4ad4cbdcb6c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-226136" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9da6bdb9ab5f93d0b0d11dfd60ab2b647380a94ce337c2314ec3504cabafa889.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=4x\\,i-3y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1de4c4c55784b4a84a0493a4cc65082c430cadcc39c3d808001b2fd27665e968.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(4x\\,i-3y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=8.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(4x\\,i-3y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =-3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=-6.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 8+2\\cdot (-6)=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420917 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "754059dd-7704-537c-8f5b-83a69628877d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420875} {:answer/id #uuid "76a9acd9-336e-553e-ac03-9d29b38dee70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420876} {:answer/id #uuid "b1bbaa21-1588-571b-867f-6c07807e4cff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420885} {:answer/id #uuid "c4eec9b3-71ac-57f5-835f-95347213cc06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420888} {:answer/id #uuid "d5afc158-099d-5fca-ba5b-ba5702d29c54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420892}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420888} :answer-field/id #uuid "e89aabf0-d356-583a-a3cf-404273cddd70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420907}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e4ef9955-c544-5a0b-9748-af661e7750b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-226115" :question/problem "![](images/57/575463a6fbe18d3f9b805e1a9c3d8e52bedd84a8db3a3c90d87381b8cb7abf74.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=-4x\\,i-4y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/eb/ebe05a522f9f46acfed63e29927b46f41d78483b5df5e7a15a15ae5f33abd2f4.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(-4x\\,i-4y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =-4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=4\\cdot (-8)=-32.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421470 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03305bbd-2ed9-5a18-8c72-9c0d0c631c78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421464} {:answer/id #uuid "78c29a36-9fe5-5900-be19-eb34c038c994" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421465} {:answer/id #uuid "97e1e9e0-3fe5-5a2d-b26c-2e7b570ac0c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421466} {:answer/id #uuid "e135fd94-1acc-52d5-ad78-5d64f964e29a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421467} {:answer/id #uuid "e888d5f3-7dc6-5267-ad8c-2c21bc41dcbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421468}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421467} :answer-field/id #uuid "600bf7ec-a1e3-56fd-89d4-4751fb47440c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f9f7d0a1-c461-5247-ab30-95b875686e93" :question/math-academy-id "q-226134" :question/problem "![](images/95/958965484cd271c5eb7ccfc8bf913386e57b9b733719885d232e562063969ca8.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=-x\\,i+2y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(-x\\,i+2y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=-8.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(-x\\,i+2y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+2\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=16.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot (-8)+2\\cdot 16=16.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421556 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c37fde0-293d-5fff-8b41-8792f1c3b2d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421550} {:answer/id #uuid "60162dda-59fc-569f-9bbc-f89a1da9b64e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421551} {:answer/id #uuid "650d25a5-3c4b-5948-9c9f-4ca92962040c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{7}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421552} {:answer/id #uuid "7a4b634a-7190-518b-8a3e-0e64b953f99a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421553} {:answer/id #uuid "f7ee88ea-070f-5d68-aaa9-8fb89bd88756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{7}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421554}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421550} :answer-field/id #uuid "a872ab99-4b04-549f-a3e0-8010a8cc8418" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "05225557-6476-5115-9d07-1e8467a995db" :question/math-academy-id "q-73631" :question/problem "![](images/39/3989e71a908c5536d97732e253effe16fadb6382a90610db0a8693eec8161393.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=5$ and the vertical radius has length $b=7.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421783 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "189bee50-4a99-5b40-af14-d79a6be4cf78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421767} {:answer/id #uuid "1ab31c93-1b65-5e8b-a761-9f8a56bd7091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421768} {:answer/id #uuid "936a7baa-180a-5ac4-87b7-254a0a451cae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421773} {:answer/id #uuid "9c1d5f42-e69e-5804-8ffc-1ba7c0a90d40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421774} {:answer/id #uuid "a21a5496-3418-514f-ae65-4dd4f8f20907" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421775}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421768} :answer-field/id #uuid "44498254-f6d6-5947-a33a-02e052e8e1fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "27b29e44-b12e-5311-b5aa-28b3cb92f7e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-161304" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨{x}^{2},\\,xy,\\,z⟩,\\,r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,2uv⟩,\\,(u,v)\\in D⊂{R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}auv+b{u}^{2}v\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}2uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2v \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2v \\\\ 1 & 2u\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2v \\\\ 0 & 2u\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2v,\\,-2u,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨{x}^{2},\\,xy,\\,z⟩{|}_{x=u\\,y=v\\,z=2uv} \\\\  & =⟨{u}^{2},\\,uv,\\,2uv⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨{u}^{2},\\,uv,\\,2uv⟩\\cdot ⟨-2v,\\,-2u,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\cdot (-2v)+uv\\cdot (-2u)+2uv\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2{u}^{2}v-2{u}^{2}v+2uv \\\\  & =2uv-4{u}^{2}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\cdot uv+(-4)\\cdot {u}^{2}v\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=2-4=-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421784 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a219dca-1e12-574f-9fc3-d709a89775ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421769} {:answer/id #uuid "4b502ee2-042d-5a3e-86ae-ba6ad14e00b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{23}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421770} {:answer/id #uuid "5be10e6b-2fc0-5157-8d4c-a6365644fb4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421771} {:answer/id #uuid "ac272ec2-a123-566b-9ae4-94a591001c16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421776} {:answer/id #uuid "bb4a967e-d128-5262-a38b-86976eb912e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{22}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421777}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421770} :answer-field/id #uuid "de26f158-7713-5413-99c7-ec6ea96881bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4c963e37-fb2b-5cec-bc6e-5cbde0fb10aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-161258" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨3x,\\,-{y}^{2},\\,2⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨3uv,\\,u,\\,-v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-1,\\,3v,\\,-3u⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe're given that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-1,\\,3v,\\,-3u⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & ={⟨3x,\\,-{y}^{2},\\,2⟩|}_{x=3uv\\,y=u\\,z=-v} \\\\  & =⟨3(3uv),\\,-{u}^{2},\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨9uv,\\,-{u}^{2},\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨9uv,\\,-{u}^{2},\\,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,3v,\\,-3u⟩ \\\\  & =9uv\\cdot (-1)+(-{u}^{2})\\cdot 3v+2\\cdot (-3u) \\\\  & =-9uv-3{u}^{2}v-6u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨3x,\\,-{y}^{2},\\,2⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-9uv-3{u}^{2}v-6u)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-9uv-3{u}^{2}v-6u)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-\\frac{9}{2}{u}^{2}v-{u}^{3}v-3{u}^{2}]}_{u=0}^{u=1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{11}{2}v-3)\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{11}{4}{v}^{2}-3v]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{4}-3 \\\\  & =-\\frac{23}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421785 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "127c5717-261e-5aef-838e-be5fab62b6d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421765} {:answer/id #uuid "176992fe-e63a-5f75-8661-ee649edc6971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421766} {:answer/id #uuid "794b5eb2-d0f5-57bd-8363-4337b7e59452" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421772} {:answer/id #uuid "cb9f4e2e-e860-55d7-8d5c-c33be0190db3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421778} {:answer/id #uuid "d7fec48c-2c4b-5a3b-8559-e99202ca21e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421779}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421765} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8009739-528a-5b9d-8b7a-977eb7894c6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f3288098-3c26-556d-b6c9-f206da47adfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-162593" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨3z,\\,-2x,\\,-y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨-v,\\,-u,\\,{u}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-1,\\,2u⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2u \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2u \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2u \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-2u,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & ={⟨3z,\\,-2x,\\,-y⟩|}_{x=-v\\,y=-u\\,z={u}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨3{u}^{2},\\,-2(-v),\\,-(-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{u}^{2},\\,2v,\\,u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨3{u}^{2},\\,2v,\\,u⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-2u,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}\\cdot 0+2v\\cdot (-2u)+u\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-4uv-u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨3z,\\,-2x,\\,-y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-4uv-u)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-4uv-u)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4uv+u)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2{u}^{2}v+\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{u=0}^{u=1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2v+\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & =-{[{v}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}v]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423051 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "364ddf2b-cdd1-5e21-88c2-478a7d637e32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+3y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423045} {:answer/id #uuid "415a3975-289f-5c0a-aa48-bc85b813f82a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423046} {:answer/id #uuid "6321cb0e-8e68-5a7b-9a36-ac871cc17b89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+2y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423047} {:answer/id #uuid "82ce7cc2-be21-5dc4-9b04-e941ddd03d24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-3y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423048} {:answer/id #uuid "ba63a292-9d83-540d-ac68-00ce4812ebb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+3y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423049}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423048} :answer-field/id #uuid "02401848-522e-501b-82ff-6e5cb3f48ab9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6f16c0b1-b80b-5067-adaf-a65b04fb7bb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-130721" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${e}^{{x}^{2}y}={y}^{2}-{x}^{3}+9.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{{x}^{2}y} & ={y}^{2}-{x}^{3}+9 \\\\ {e}^{{x}^{2}y}-{y}^{2}+{x}^{3} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}y}-{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=9.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{{x}^{2}y}-{y}^{2}+{x}^{3})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{{x}^{2}y}-{y}^{2}+{x}^{3})j \\\\  & =(2xy{e}^{{x}^{2}y}+3{x}^{2})i+({x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}y}-2y)j \\\\ ∇f(2,0) & =(2(2)(0){e}^{({2}^{2})(0)}+3({2}^{2}))i+(({2}^{2}){e}^{({2}^{2})(0)}-2(0))j \\\\  & =12\\,i+4\\,j \\\\  & =⟨12,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(2,0)=⟨12,4⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{12} & =\\frac{y-0}{4} \\\\ 4(x-2) & =12y \\\\ x-2 & =3y \\\\ x-3y-2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
